# National Awards of India: Bharat Ratna to the Gallantry Ladder

> India's honours system runs on two ladders — civilian and military —
> and the general-awareness paper walks both. The Bharat Ratna sits at
> the top of one; the Param Vir Chakra at the top of the other; and
> between them the Padma trio, the peacetime Ashoka Chakra series, and
> the cultural and sports honours. The examiner's favourite move is to
> swap a name between the ladders.

## 1. The Bharat Ratna and the Padma Trio

[definition]
The **Bharat Ratna** (Jewel of India), instituted in **1954**, is the
highest civilian award of the Republic, conferred "for exceptional
service of the highest order" without distinction of race,
occupation, position, or sex. It carries no monetary grant, and since
2011 it has been open to *any* field of endeavour — earlier it was
limited to arts, literature, science, and public service.
[/definition]

The first recipients (1954) were three: **C. Rajagopalachari, Dr. S.
Radhakrishnan, and Sir C.V. Raman**. Notable "first" categories the
exams quote: first woman — **Indira Gandhi (1971)**; first
foreigner — **Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (1987)**; second foreigner and
first non-Indian head of state — **Nelson Mandela (1990)**; first
sportsperson — **Sachin Tendulkar (2014)**, then chess champion
**Viswanathan Anand** declined consideration in the same season; and
first musician — **M.S. Subbulakshmi (1998)**. The award may be
awarded posthumously — Rajiv Gandhi (1991) being the standard
example.

| Award | Rank | Instituted | Field |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bharat Ratna | Highest civilian | 1954 | Any field (since 2011) |
| Padma Vibhushan | 2nd highest civilian | 1954 | Exceptional and distinguished service |
| Padma Bhushan | 3rd highest civilian | 1954 | Distinguished service of high order |
| Padma Shri | 4th highest civilian | 1954 | Distinguished service |

All three Padma awards were also instituted in 1954 — a "which came
first" trap the answer to which is "they are the same year." The
**Bharat Ratna can be suspended**, as it famously was in 1977–80 and
1992 (the short-lived tableau around the posthumous award to
Netaji), but it has never been withdrawn from a living recipient.

## 2. The Gallantry Ladder — Wartime and Peacetime

| Award | Position | For | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Param Vir Chakra (PVC) | Highest wartime gallantry | Enemy action in wartime | Equivalent pre-independence: Victoria Cross |
| Ashoka Chakra | Highest peacetime gallantry | Conspicuous courage otherwise than in the face of the enemy | Often awarded to soldiers and police in counter-terror duty |
| Kirti Chakra | 2nd peacetime | — | — |
| Shaurya Chakra | 3rd peacetime | — | — |
| Maha Vir Chakra | 2nd wartime | — | — |
| Vir Chakra | 3rd wartime | — | — |
| Sena / Nau Sena / Vayu Sena Medals | Service gallantry | Army / Navy / Air Force | — |

The exam pattern is the crossover: **"highest peacetime gallantry
award" = Ashoka Chakra**, while the PVC belongs to wartime. The
peacetime series is named for Ashoka, the wartime series retains the
older V-word ladder (Param Vir, Maha Vir, Vir) inherited from the
VC-era vocabulary.

## 3. Literary, Film, and Scientific Honours

The **Jnanpith Award** (1965) is India's highest literary honour,
first awarded to the Malayalam writer **G. Sankara Kurup**. The
**Sahitya Akademi Award** (1954) honours individual works in 24
languages. For cinema, the **Dadasaheb Phalke Award** (1969) is the
highest film honour, first conferred on **Devika Rani**; the National
Film Awards themselves are administered by the Directorate of Film
Festivals. In science, the **Shanti Swarup Bhatnagar Prize** (1958)
is the country's highest science award, given by CSIR to researchers
under 45.

| Field | Highest honour | First recipient / year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Literature | Jnanpith Award | G. Sankara Kurup (1965) |
| Literature (work-wise) | Sahitya Akademi Award | 1954 |
| Cinema | Dadasaheb Phalke Award | Devika Rani (1969) |
| Science | Shanti Swarup Bhatnagar Prize | 1958 |
| Sports | Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna | Viswanathan Anand (1991–92) |

## 4. The Sports Honours

The **Khel Ratna**, renamed the **Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna** in
2021, is India's highest sporting honour (instituted 1991–92, first
conferred on Viswanathan Anand). Below it sit the **Arjuna Award**
(1961) for consistent outstanding performance, the **Dronacharya
Award** (1985) for coaches, and the **Dhyan Chand Award** (2002) for
lifetime contribution to sports — the last named after the hockey
wizard whose birthday, **29 August**, is National Sports Day.

A modern detail worth noting: from 2023 the sports ministry renamed
several honours after Major Dhyan Chand — the Khel Ratna being the
headline case — so older coaching material still says "Rajiv Gandhi
Khel Ratna," which is now wrong in the option list it no longer
appears in.

[example]
The crossover trap in action: "Which is India's highest peacetime
gallantry award?" — options: Param Vir Chakra, Ashoka Chakra, Vir
Chakra, Kirti Chakra. The instinct is to mark the PVC because it is
the most famous; the answer is the Ashoka Chakra, the peacetime
mirror. The reverse question asks the highest *wartime* decoration,
and there the PVC is right. Fix the two ladders as parallel columns:
wartime (Param–Maha–Vir) and peacetime (Ashoka–Kirti–Shaurya).
[/example]

[summary]
Civilian ladder: Bharat Ratna (1954; first trio Rajagopalachari,
Radhakrishnan, Raman; first woman Indira Gandhi 1971; first
sportsperson Sachin Tendulkar 2014) over the Padma trio (Vibhushan,
Bhushan, Shri — all also 1954). Gallantry ladders: wartime Param Vir
Chakra and peacetime Ashoka Chakra at the top, with Maha Vir / Vir
and Kirti / Shaurya below. Specialised tops: Jnanpith (literature,
first G. Sankara Kurup 1965), Dadasaheb Phalke (cinema, Devika Rani
1969), Bhatnagar (science, 1958), and the Major Dhyan Chand Khel
Ratna (sports, first Viswanathan Anand 1991–92, renamed 2021). When
two awards both say "highest," the missing words are always
"wartime" versus "peacetime."
[/summary]
